  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nSurface high pressure overhead the Great Lakes region is making for \na beautiful end to the work week today with lower humidity, \nseasonable temperatures, and some sunshine. High level clouds can be \nseen advecting northward on visible satellite imagery but dry \nconditions are expected to prevail today and tonight. Light \nsoutheasterly winds and WAA will boost temperatures into the upper \n70s to low 80s this afternoon. \n\nA stationary front currently centered over the Ohio River Valley \nwill be the focus for 40-60% chances for rain and storms on \nSaturday. Scattered showers are likely with potentially a \nthunderstorm or two on Saturday, mainly for the southeastern half of \nour forecast area. This feature will lift north as a warm front on \nSaturday with an attendant 500mb shortwave. Then, an upper level \ntrough and surface cold front will move through the Great Lakes \nregion later Saturday evening. Increased WAA and moisture transport \nalongside lift from the warm front will spark chances for showers \nand storms, especially along and south of US 24 in Indiana and \nOhio. Scattered showers are expected to begin before daybreak \nbetween 06- 09Z Saturday and end around 18-21Z). Severe weather \nis not expected at this time, however, an isolated storm or two \nwill be possible due to surface based instability of up to 1000 \nJ/kg by midday Saturday. Shear and low/mid level lapse rates \nwill be too weak for any organized severe weather. Northwest \nIndiana and southwest Lower Michigan will likely remain dry, as \nthe cold front likely passes through early Saturday morning \nprior to peak diurnal heating. High temperatures will be in the \nlow to mid 80s area-wide on Saturday.\n\nStay weather aware if you have plans to head to Lake Michigan on \nSunday! Behind the cold frontal passage on Saturday, waves are \nexpected to increase for southeastern Lake Michigan beaches in \nBerrien and La Porte counties. CAA and northwesterly flow Sunday \nbehind the front will yield onshore flow and waves of 2-3 ft, \nmeaning the swim risk will be Moderate for both La Porte and Berrien \ncounties on Sunday. Breaking waves and currents are expected. \n\nThe rest of the weekend into early next week appears dry, with low \nchances (20%) for rain/storms late Sunday into Monday morning with a \nshortwave and associated upper level trough that passes through the \nGreat Lakes. With a potentially closed upper level low building \nacross the Pacific Northwest, this allows yet another upper level \nridge to amplify across the central and eastern CONUS early next \nweek. Surface high pressure builds over the eastern US on Tuesday \ntoo, with return flow and moisture from the Gulf ushering in warmer \nthan normal temperatures and humid conditions underneath the peak of \nthe upper level ridge over the Great Lakes. Another frontal system \nlikely approaches late Tuesday and Wednesday, with additional \nchances for rain and storms (30-60%). This system may be slow moving \nor potentially even stall to our northwest (still mixed model \nguidance results on if it actually comes through our area or not), \ndepending on how strong the upper level ridge is. There are no clear \nsigns of fall in any of the long range guidance as our forecast area \nlooks to remain sandwiched between hot and humid air to our south \nand cooler, drier air to our north for the foreseeable future.",
